// src/kernel/core/syscall/sys_taskinfo.pdx — R57.M4-003 (paideia-os #1799)
//
// SYS_TASKINFO — ring-3 task-table introspection syscall body (sysno 83).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scope
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The M4 milestone item R57.M4-003 asks for /bin/ps + the syscall that
// backs it.  This file lands the SYSCALL BODY (the kernel-side function
// the dispatch shim tail-calls into).  The dispatch shim itself lives in
// src/kernel/core/syscall/dispatch.pdx §dispatch_taskinfo (see the sibling
// #741-pattern shims dispatch_read / dispatch_pdxfs_dir_readnext for the
// two canonical layouts — the shim here mirrors dispatch_pdxfs_dir_readnext
// byte-for-byte modulo the record size).
//
// This file is placed at src/kernel/core/syscall/ (NOT under handlers/)
// following the R53.M2-002 sys_mount precedent — the "one file per syscall
// at the top of the syscall namespace" convention for new (R51+) syscalls
// that participate in a broader design bundle.  Older handlers (sys_read,
// sys_write, sys_open) live under handlers/ because they predate that
// convention; the split is history, not policy.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Contract summary
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sys_taskinfo_body(idx, kernel_dst) -> u64
//   Kernel-only entry point (no user pointers; the shim has already
//   validated the user buffer and passed a kernel-side .bss scratch).
//   Called by the dispatch shim AND by kernel-side witnesses that
//   exercise the body directly.
//
//   rdi = idx           in [0, MAX_PIDS) — an index into the task pool
//                       (NOT a pid; idx N corresponds to pid == N because
//                       _pid_table is pid-indexed and pid 0 is a reserved
//                       sentinel, so idx 0 naturally returns -ENOENT).
//   rsi = kernel_dst    kernel VA of a 32-byte destination buffer.
//   -> rax = 0            success — 32-byte record written to dst.
//            -ESRCH (-3)  idx >= MAX_PIDS; iteration should stop.
//            -ENOENT (-2) idx is in-range but the slot is empty.
//
// The record layout is defined in src/kernel/core/sched/task_info.pdx §Record
// layout — this file does not re-declare it, and the body below is a
// single-instruction wrapper around task_get_info() so a future record-layout
// evolution (M1 comm[16] wiring) affects only the primitive.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the body is a thin wrapper — layer discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every write to _pid_table + the pid → slab arithmetic lives behind
// task_slab_of_pid + pid_alive + task_get_info in src/kernel/core/sched/.
// The syscall body's job is exactly one thing the read-primitive is NOT
// asked to do: participate in the syscall dispatch shape (SysV register
// contract, rsp alignment discipline, error-sentinel forwarding).
// Everything else — the record layout, the bounds check, the pid-table
// probe, the field copies — belongs to the primitive.
//
// Concretely this means:
//
//   (a) A future /proc-style KERNEL SERVICE (not a syscall — an internal
//       consumer like a panic-dump "who was running" line) calls
//       task_get_info() directly and inherits every arg-shape decision
//       for free.
//   (b) A change to what the record contains (M1 comm[16] wiring)
//       touches ONE file (task_info.pdx) and this body's contract stays
//       identical.  The syscall's userspace ABI is a stable 32-byte
//       payload whose interpretation is documented at the primitive.
//   (c) A witness that wants to exercise the read path WITHOUT going
//       through dispatch (kernel-side test fixture) calls task_get_info()
//       and never has to reason about SysV shuffle or scratch buffers.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Effect row — why {mem} @{} and not {mem, sysreg} @{cap}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The R57.M4-003 issue text quotes an effect row of `!{mem, sysreg} @{cap}`
// — that row describes the SYSCALL PATH as a whole (kernel entry via sysret
// touches MSRs; the future capability policy will gate this read).  The
// BODY function itself is a leaf that does register-to-register + a tiny
// number of memory reads/writes, so its effect row is honestly `!{mem} @{}`:
//
//   !{mem}   — reads _pid_table + task_struct fields; writes the 32B record.
//   !{}      — no MSR / CR touches (the syscall entry did the sysret /
//              CR3 flip before dispatch runs; the body sees a clean SysV
//              call).
//   @{}      — no capability check inside the body.  The gate the M1+
//              policy adds lives at the dispatch shim (rdi = current-TCB's
//              cap slot, verify against KIND_TASKINFO with R_INTROSPECT
//              rights) BEFORE the body is called; the body itself is
//              unchanged.  This matches sys_clock_read_ns_body's own posture
//              (src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/clock_read_ns.pdx §55):
//              a leaf read that consumes no capability inside its own frame.
//
// The dispatch shim's declared effect row (in dispatch.pdx §syscall_dispatch)
// already unions `sysreg` in for exactly this reason.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Cross-references
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   src/kernel/core/sched/task_info.pdx     — the read primitive
//   src/kernel/core/sched/task_pool.pdx     — task_slab_of_pid + pid_alive
//   src/kernel/core/syscall/dispatch.pdx    — dispatch_taskinfo shim
//   src/user/ps.pdx                         — /bin/ps M0 caller

module SysTaskinfo = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_taskinfo_body(idx, kernel_dst) -> u64 in rax.
  //
  // Alignment: entry rsp %16 == 8 (SysV; dispatch shim called us with a
  // `call sys_taskinfo_body` after its own #672 pad).  The nested
  // `call task_get_info` requires rsp %16 == 0 at the call boundary
  // (SysV callee sees %16 == 8 at entry).  We add the standard 8-byte
  // pad before the call and remove it after — same bracket
  // sys_clock_read_ns_body uses (src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/
  // clock_read_ns.pdx §61).
  //
  // No register marshaling: task_get_info takes (rdi=idx, rsi=kernel_dst),
  // which is exactly what SysV places for us on entry.  The body is a
  // pure forwarder — every substantive decision lives in the primitive.
  //
  // Return value: task_get_info's rax verbatim.  0 on success; -ENOENT
  // (-2 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE) for an empty slot; -ESRCH (-3 =
  // 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD) past the end.  The dispatch shim's writeback
  // step (user_write_bytes_via_walk) fires only when rax == 0.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_taskinfo_body : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) (kernel_dst: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem},
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R57.M4-003 (#1799): sys_taskinfo syscall body (sysno 83). Thin forwarder onto task_get_info in src/kernel/core/sched/task_info.pdx — the primitive owns the record layout, the bounds check, and the pid-table probe, so a future record-shape change (M1 comm[16] wiring per task_info.pdx §Name field) affects one file and this body stays byte-identical. No arg marshal: task_get_info's SysV contract matches ours exactly. Standard #672 8-byte alignment pad brackets the nested call so task_get_info sees rsp %16 == 0 at its own call boundary. Effect row {mem} @{} rather than the syscall path's {mem, sysreg} @{cap} because the body is a leaf: the sysret and CR3 flip happened at syscall_entry before dispatch ran; the capability policy the M1+ posture will introduce lives at the dispatch shim (verify current-TCB's cap slot against KIND_TASKINFO with R_INTROSPECT rights BEFORE calling this body), matching sys_clock_read_ns_body's own leaf-read posture at src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/clock_read_ns.pdx §55. Return values are task_get_info's rax verbatim: 0 (success — record written), -ENOENT (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE, empty slot), -ESRCH (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD, past end). The dispatch shim's user_write_bytes_via_walk writeback fires only on 0.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;                       // #672 alignment pad
        call task_get_info;               // rax = 0 | -ENOENT | -ESRCH
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

}
